\documentclass[11pt]{article}
\usepackage[margin=1in]{geometry}

\title{Dust Soiling on Rooftop Arrays:\newline A Fourteen-Month Record from a Semi-Arid Campus}
\author{R.~Cabetas, N.~Iyer, and T.~Olawale}
\date{June 2025}

\begin{document}
\maketitle

\begin{abstract}
A monitoring campaign on the engineering hall rooftop ran from March 2024 to May 2025.
The median daily soiling loss was 0.35~\% per day across the fourteen-month record.
Two haboob events in April 2025 accounted for nearly half of the annual loss.
Weekly manual cleaning recovered most of the lost yield within one day.
\end{abstract}

\section{Introduction}
Dust soiling is a persistent loss mechanism for photovoltaic arrays in dry climates.
Campus rooftops are attractive sites for such arrays because the load is close at hand.
Soiling losses on tilted rooftop arrays are less studied than those on utility-scale plants.
The campus estate office asked for a local estimate before approving a second array.
This report summarizes fourteen months of continuous monitoring on the engineering hall.

\section{Site and Instrumentation}
The array on the engineering hall consists of 24 polycrystalline modules rated at 310~W each.
Modules face south at a tilt of eighteen degrees.
A reference cell was mounted beside the array and cleaned every morning.
Soiling ratio was logged at one-minute resolution by a pair of optical sensors.
Rainfall and wind were recorded by the rooftop weather mast.
The data logger was housed in the lift motor room and serviced monthly.

\section{Cleaning Protocol}
The estate staff washed the array every Friday morning with deionized water.
Each wash was entered in a paper logbook with start and end times.
Extra washes were called after dust storms at the duty officer's discretion.
No detergents were used during the campaign.
Logbook pages were photographed and filed by the research assistant each month.

\section{Soiling Measurements}
The median daily soiling loss was 0.35~\% per day across the full record.
Losses exceeded 1.2~\% per day only during the two haboob events.
The steepest single-day drop followed the haboob of 9 April 2025.
Recovery after a scheduled wash was typically complete within one day.
Sensor drift stayed below the calibration threshold for the whole campaign.

\section{Seasonal Patterns}
Spring months carried the highest loss rates, driven by airborne dust from the west lots.
The winter monsoon weeks showed near-zero soiling and occasional self-cleaning by rain.
Summer losses tracked the irrigation schedule of the sports fields downwind.
Autumn leaf fall shaded two modules for part of October and was logged separately.

\section{Conclusion}
The fourteen-month record shows that dust soiling is a first-order loss for rooftop arrays on this campus.
Weekly manual cleaning kept the median loss low and cost little staff time.
The monitoring design proved cheap and easy to run.
\end{document}
